{"step_index":59,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T20:13:29Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-22T01:43:29+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-22T01:43:36+05:30\nThe search for \"best way to fine-tune Qwen 2.5 Coder 3B 2025 2026 Unsloth vs Axolotl vs TRL SFT trainer\" returned the following summary:\nTo determine the best way to fine-tune **Qwen 2.5 Coder 3B** in 2026, you should choose your framework based on your specific infrastructure and project needs. The consensus among current development practices is as follows:\n\n### Quick Decision Guide\n| Framework | Best For | Why? |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Unsloth** | **Single-GPU / Efficiency** | Provides the fastest training speeds and lowest VRAM usage (up to 80% reduction) via optimized CUDA/Triton kernels. |\n| **Axolotl** | **Production / Multi-GPU** | Offers high flexibility, configuration-driven (YAML) workflows, and robust support for distributed/multi-GPU training. |\n| **TRL (SFTTrainer)** | **Research / Custom Control** | The underlying reference implementation. Best if you need raw control or are building custom training loops. |\n\n---\n\n### Why Unsloth is Often the Top Choice for 3B Models\nFor a model as compact as Qwen 2.5 Coder 3B, **Unsloth** is widely considered the most efficient starting point for most users[1][2].\n*   **VRAM Efficiency:** Because 3B models are already lightweight, Unsloth’s ability to drastically reduce VRAM overhead allows you to use much larger batch sizes or longer sequence lengths on modest consumer hardware (even on free-tier Colab instances)[3][4][5].\n*   **Speed:** It typically achieves 2x or greater speedups over standard Hugging Face/TRL setups by rewriting backward passes and key attention operations[6][4].\n*   **Ease of Use:** It integrates directly with the `SFTTrainer` from Hugging Face’s `trl` library, meaning you get the speed of custom kernels while maintaining a familiar, Python-native workflow[3][7].\n\n### When to Choose Others\n*   **Choose Axolotl** if your project is moving from experimentation to production. It excels at managing complex, multi-stage training pipelines and distributed training\n<truncated 3605 bytes>\npbDrQrTNYnU-rFAvHFumYNQ-KdkAAiqm939wJljJw2)\n[9] [futureagi.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEtkrDrlPsCxD8nCuaTydR11KsbO_yshZP90HG5suHFU7AtVmfTPx99xg4EmeJ83K0uglkw-RtwUnJeMF9IbMo5AGSFjlEES_Sk3Wr7knDKrGBLaT_2tYJ1F2mxrFe2Gl1-O4rmnnKGlvhagPI=)\n[10] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFar-t0qdX2oAxY5klIBayy6wxIgW-t0X8xsGD3paHDrL5n9SEyGHyy7SvvMBC5UqBDMk7dasqYqof0cDZOAoM5buGGG7qDQ-bU7VirprYr3OKlYgY0HpL5Nv0z2AtuItEwqY4QP4oLgX5IwLpSHM7Xmqaq7t9RDUcKqAOgVc58Y_rxRn6bqKJetYBs4C5ImZh-XV11Mp81zloq4bcD)\n[11] [daily.dev](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFkRjSHb39prpf8UIaubsCqZQWSkOgYYjylt4eXSMyPC_D-7JyE3Lx4NFQ1-1rCjhLYCWTfKMZ5K0Tlyx0y_JpP3ORFogu3ThUEoOxAmi8wPHirgYetIxtHwb2dkS6L9Z-4PHy7o3I8h0hsdt4_zbAyl6DMuzq7oLddxkK5624ZAj9xFi_LvrmzBkjM7pVF_FyPgRo=)\n[12] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE3MzVFrhtXtzVE9ym8PaJRwA7ekGbxuzz9oep0pIwHOP5R0yKkDDW1jMGmzS1QRSzxXKgNyZWAEcIPKbhpn-W7hndZQWWEhZzn3WUvm8j_sW-TZH11Sn4mZdxit_X3V4kRtfsqtBy80VvNXgOo4zqoYnSBCiBTZzJt)\n[13] [f22labs.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFfSTn4d047nMzG8i5qjuPXP4vpm3u2zn7SwdZNMvAONpDWMC_8_DDDC-VKIW7t3rDhrUa8V9YCOP2XBV1VLff9JEJs_1UPuITDrVbm4-Hq-g5hHukTw0c8MJFbewIB4a72pfW_9lRf1KSSBtFJe63oLBW1cFZm4UXsrGKEQuFphMJb1A==)\n[14] [towardsai.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG7DqU1d4wbEO3Sr6GpALgsN1cVfTQICBAfn7AMl5f1_nm8uLWWtYJXbPGFSf1Wva0oq1nxhIfnvGJs3zi9MIwFskvroYq0T9nX2pmYz09YYzTjO6OMOmFCgqNezTDkOk_S9w8T9jsnJHs3thqEbsB3nOdq7WYTfVI3QIJIqN8P-Spv-ypRFQqlr3mf2iYoqMt1ShwrS2YHao-tZvyP0fprLvaVIzyvvxEBWSBF9aDkfprGyw==)\n[15] [towardsai.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-